{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>When you draw near to a city to fight against it, etc.</b> The verse speaks of an optional war (מלחמת הרשות), as is explicit in the matter (Deuteronomy 20:15), \"Thus you shall do to all the cities that are very far from you\" — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 20:10). The Rav wrote that this is from the Sifrei (Shofetim 199), for they taught there in this language: \"The verse speaks of an optional war.\" But the intent of our Sages in this verse is only to say that the passage at its end distinguishes between the two types of wars. However, the call for peace applies even in a commanded war (מלחמת מצוה), for they are obligated to call for peace even to the seven nations, since Moses called for peace to Sihon king of the Amorites and did not transgress the positive commandment and the negative commandment in this passage — \"you shall utterly destroy them\" (Deuteronomy 20:17) and \"you shall not let any soul live\" (Deuteronomy 20:16). But the difference between them, when they do not make peace and wage war, is that the verse commanded concerning the distant cities to strike every male and to keep alive for themselves the women and children among the males, whereas in the cities of these nations He commanded to destroy even the women and children. And thus our Sages said in Midrash Devarim Rabbah (5:13), and it is also in Tanhuma and in the Jerusalem Talmud (Shevi'it 6:1): Rabbi Shemuel son of Rabbi Nachmani said, \"Joshua son of Nun fulfilled this passage. What did Joshua do? He would send a proclamation (פרוסדיטגמא) to every place he went to conquer, and he would write in it: whoever seeks to make peace, let him come and make peace; whoever seeks to leave, let him leave; whoever seeks to wage war, let him wage war. The Girgashite departed. The Gibeonites, who made peace — Joshua made peace with them. The thirty-one kings who came to fight — the Holy One, blessed be He, struck them down,\" etc. And thus the verse says concerning all of them (Joshua 11:19–20), \"There was not a city that made peace with the children of Israel except the Hivvite inhabitants of Gibeon; all the rest they took in battle. For it was from YHWH to strengthen their hearts to meet the battle against Israel, in order to destroy them\" — by inference, if they had wanted to make peace, they would have made peace with them.\n\nAnd it appears that there is yet another difference in the request for peace: in the distant cities, peace is requested from them — that they be tributary to us and serve us — whereas in the cities of these nations, peace and tribute and servitude are requested from them on condition that they accept upon themselves not to worship idolatry. And the verse did not mention this in this passage, because it was already forbidden to us concerning idolaters (Exodus 23:33), \"They shall not dwell in your land, lest they cause you to sin against Me, for you will serve their elohim.\" And it is possible that there was no need to inform them except of peace and tribute and servitude, and after they become subservient to us, we inform them that we administer justice concerning idolatry and its worshippers, whether individuals or multitudes.\n\nAnd likewise what is said here (Deuteronomy 20:18), \"So that they will not teach you to do according to all their abominations\" — they said in the Sifrei (Shofetim 202): this means, if they repented they are not killed. This refers to the seven nations, and the repentance is that they accept upon themselves the seven commandments that the children of Noah were commanded — not that they convert to become righteous gerim. And in tractate Sotah (35a) they said that the Torah was written on the stones in seventy languages, and they wrote at the bottom, \"So that they will not teach you to do\" — this means, if they repented, they accept them. And Rashi explained there, to inform the nations dwelling outside the border of the land of Israel — that we were not commanded to destroy them, but only those within the borders, so that they not teach them their corrupt practices; but those dwelling outside it, they say to them: if you repent, we will accept you. But those within it, they do not accept them, because their repentance would be out of fear — and this is the language of the Rav. And it is not correct, for concerning the cities of these nations which YHWH your Elohim gives you as an inheritance, it is regarding them that He said <b>\"so that they will not teach you\"</b> — meaning that if they repented, they are not killed. And likewise He said concerning them (Exodus 23:33), \"They shall not dwell in your land, lest they cause you to sin against Me, for you will serve their elohim\" — this means, if they forsook their elohim, it is permitted for them to dwell there. And this is the matter that King Solomon did, of whom it is written (I Kings 9:15–22): \"And this is the account of the levy which King Solomon raised to build the house of YHWH and his own house and the Millo and the wall of Jerusalem,\" etc. \"All the people remaining from the Amorite, the Hittite, the Perizzite, the Hivvite, and the Jebusite, who were left after them in the land, whom the children of Israel could not destroy — Solomon made them a levy of slaves until this day. But of the children of Israel Solomon did not make slaves.\" And this matter he did in accordance with the Torah, for they had accepted upon themselves the seven commandments. And it is a clear matter, since Solomon made them do his labor — his hand had dominion over them and he could have destroyed them, except that it was permitted to keep them alive, as we have written."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "The Rav cited is Maimonides; the passage referenced is likely Mishneh Torah, Hilchot Melachim 6:1. Rashi's interpretation disputed here is from Sotah 35b (s.v. כדי שלא ילמדו)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:52:40.283592681Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}